WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.520
Mō tātou, a, mō ka uri a muri ake nei

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.220
Since the beginning of the year, the roading
work in the Regeneration areas has been going

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.500
ahead full steam, and construction of the
new Kaiapoi East road link is well underway.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.320
It's basically a replacement of Cass Street
and Feldwick Drive, and it cuts across the

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.320
Regeneration area from the vincinity of the
Jones/Cass intersection.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.440
They've been setting up site over the last week or so

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.200
but there's now a digger stripping out for the new road.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.040
So that job will be going through to the middle of June.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.679
Following the discovery and treatment of Asbestos
under the road surface, progress across the

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.600
river in Kaiapoi South area is also good.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.040
This is the reconstruction of Courtenay Drive.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.680
It's the last project to be done in the area here

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.560
and we've already been through and fixed the sewers,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.960
and the scheme involves re-narrowing the carriage-way

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.680
and putting in a few features that will hopefully control vehicle speeds as we come through.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.720
Making good progress, and we hope to be finished early to mid May.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.160
In this update, we’ll be talking about the the Regeneration areas in Kairaki and The Pines Beach.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.480
Most of this land will be divested to the Te Kōhaka o Tūhaitara Trust

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.640
and they're currently consulting with the community on their plans for this area.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.880
We as a trust, count it quite a priviledge to actually have been given these Regeneration areas

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.880
to bring into the park.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.480
So, they're going to come into Tūhaitara Coastal Park.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.080
It's a 50-50 trust between Ngāi Tahu and the Waimakariri District Council

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.840
and the good bit about that is that it belongs to the people of New Zealand.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.200
So we want to ensure by enhancing what is already here

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:56.040
that we are also improving, returning and doing what we can to make people want to use something here.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.040
And we look at this as being the jewel in the crown of the Waimakariri District.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.640
We're aiming to bring a lot more vitality back to the area and to encourage more people to be here.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.800
Because the park stretches from the Waimakariri River to the Rakahuri, the Ashley,

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.800
we actually need an entrance.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:23.760
So we're proposing an identified entrance, maybe an educational facility in the same area.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.560
So we've developed relationships with various schools and the University Of Canterbury.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.880
We've got plans for a community garden and a Rongoā garden,

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:37.400
so, Rongoā is natural medicines using native species.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.400
Over here we've got part of the park itself, the southern-most boundary of the park.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:47.120
We've got pine trees that have been planted to stabilise the dunes.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.720
But they're coming up on their rotation for harvest,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:58.960
and so the replanting, there will be an element of pine forest replaced in there to continue that dune protection,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.080
there's also the opportunity for us to plant some native species, some podocarps

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:12.000
to provide interest, provide setbacks for walking trails and also for carbon sequestration.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.920
In Kairaki, the regeneration areas are interspersed
with privately owned properties.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.240
The trust have some ideas on how this land could be used.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:26.320
Environmentally-friendly, self-contained, perhaps off-grid styled accommodation,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.880
the tiny home movement, so stuff that sits lightly on the landscape.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.880
Native plantings throughout the area once again.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.280
Biodiversity attracts more birds, makes it a much nicer and enjoyable environment

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.600
for the people who already live here, for the people who utilise the campground,

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.480
for the people who utilise either the river or the park itself.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.430
The plan is open for consultation until the 14th of April  and full details of the proposal

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.360
are available on the Trust’s website.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.560
We don't have a plan that we're just going to foist onto the community

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.320
and say this is what we're going to do and this is what you're going to get.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.160
You've got an opportunity to actually say what you want to see.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.800
Log in, have a look, see what we're about, provide some feedback.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.560
There's no bad ideas, some are just more feasible than others.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.240
In the next video we’ll updating you on progress in the Kaiapoi Marine Precinct

